### `pdf_export.py`

Builds a PDF from a research result using `fpdf2`.

- **Unicode font handling**: searches common system paths for DejaVu Sans (bundled with most
  Linux distros). If found, uses it so non-Latin text (Arabic, Cyrillic, etc.) renders instead
  of crashing. If not found, falls back to stripping unsupported characters and adds a small
  note at the bottom of the PDF explaining why.
- **`write_cell()`**: a wrapper around `multi_cell` that (a) uses character-level line wrapping
  so a long unbroken string like a URL never raises fpdf2's "not enough horizontal space"
  error, and (b) explicitly resets the cursor to the left margin before writing — without this,
  a prior `multi_cell` call can leave the cursor near the page's right edge, which combined with
  character-wrapping causes an infinite loop trying to fit a character into ~0mm of space.
- **`write_structured_text()`**: parses the model's markdown output (`## headers`, `- bullets`,
  `1. numbered lists`, `**bold**` sub-headers) into actual visual formatting — larger/bold text
  for headers, `•` prefixes for bullets — instead of printing literal `##`/`-` characters on
  the page.

---

### `docx_export.py`

Builds a real Word document using `python-docx`.

- **`_add_structured_text()`**: the same markdown-parsing logic as the PDF exporter, but
  producing genuine Word elements instead of visual approximations — actual `Heading 1`–`4`
  paragraph styles, `List Bullet` / `List Number` styles, and bold text runs. This means the
  resulting `.docx` has real document structure: opening it in Word and using the navigation
  pane / table-of-contents features actually works, because the headings are real heading
  styles, not just bold text that happens to look like one.
- Document layout: title (Heading 0) → Summary (Heading 1 + body) → Full Report (Heading 1 +
  parsed structure) → Sources (bulleted list) → Tools Used (paragraph).

