## Key Design Decisions (the "why")

**Why is the Groq fallback split into two separate calls (research, then reformat) instead of
one combined call like the primary Gemini agent?**
Smaller/faster models (Groq's Llama family) are noticeably less reliable at *simultaneously*
calling tools correctly **and** hitting an exact JSON schema in the same turn — this produced
recurring "malformed tool call" errors. Splitting the work into two single-purpose calls (research
freely in plain text, then separately reformat that text into JSON with no tools in scope at all)
removes the competition between those two jobs and fixed the failure mode at its root, rather than
just retrying the same fragile combined call.

**Why are fallback agents a list of separate `AgentExecutor` objects instead of using LangChain's
`.with_fallbacks()`?**
`create_tool_calling_agent()` calls `.bind_tools()` internally, which `RunnableWithFallbacks`
doesn't support cleanly — using it would have silently broken tool calling rather than actually
providing a working fallback. Building fully separate executors is more code, but is the version
that's actually reliable.

**Why override `tools_used` after parsing instead of trusting the model's own JSON field?**
Models can hallucinate what "tools" means contextually (e.g. listing Python libraries as tools
when asked about Python) instead of reporting which of the three real tools (search/wikipedia/save)
it actually invoked. Reading the agent's own execution log (`intermediate_steps`) is a
deterministic source of truth that doesn't depend on the model accurately self-reporting.

**Why does the storage layer auto-detect Supabase vs. SQLite instead of requiring a config flag?**
So the same code works immediately for local development (zero setup — SQLite just works) and
for production/multi-device use (set two environment variables, no code change) — the presence
of `SUPABASE_URL`/`SUPABASE_KEY` alone determines behavior.

**Why is deep research mode a separate function (`run_deep_research`) rather than a parameter on
`run_research`?**
The two have fundamentally different call shapes — one LLM call vs. many sequential ones with a
synthesis step at the end — and conflating them into one function with branching logic would have
made both harder to reason about and test independently.

